ound she was known for  always looking very ladylike and she  loved the social aspects that went along  with being a leading community in the  Newark era but again she was the first  female head of a major commercial bank  and then became the first woman governor  of the Amer  and Stock Exchange so I found her career  it's kind of a paradigm of that era 1950  like the Brooklyn Bridge bridging  different spans if you will the 1950  context of a woman belongs at home  versus coming changes of getting more  involved into the corporate world okay  that's about it I thank everybody and  I'll be available if anyone has anything  else that they'd like to bring up thank  you  [Applause]
 

